<TopicPage slug="subjects/dance/foundational/performance-technique-and-expression">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Dance performance is the disciplined union of two different achievements: executing movement
    accurately (physical and technical skill) and making it mean something (expressive skill). A
    dancer can have perfect technique and be invisible on stage; the performance skills — focus,
    musicality, projection, communication — are what the audience actually receives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five layers of performance" widget="equationStep" config={perfCfg} />

  <h2>Technique is style-specific</h2>
  <p>
    "Good technique" means something different in ballet (turnout from the hips, pointed lines,
    verticality), contemporary (release, weight-sharing, floorwork) and street styles (groove,
    isolation, musicality). The grammar of each form defines correctness — which is why dancers
    train across styles precisely to widen what their bodies can say.
  </p>

  <h2>Safe practice: anatomy applied</h2>
  <p>
    Dance is an athletic career with an artistic calendar, and its injuries cluster where anatomy
    is ignored: forced turnout torquing knees, landing loads on unprepared ankles, under-fuelled
    bodies. Safe dance practice is applied anatomy — warm-ups that prepare exactly the tissues
    about to work, progressive loading, rest — and it is examinable, not optional.
  </p>

  <h2>Rehearsal is where performance is built</h2>
  <p>
    The stage moment is the visible 1%: performance is assembled in rehearsal through repetition
    with intention — marking for memory, running for stamina, and the final transformation from
    "steps" to communication. Dancers learn to rehearse presence itself: where the eyes go, how a
    phrase breathes, when to give the movement away to a partner. Technique fills the body;
    rehearsal teaches it to speak.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AQA GCSE Dance 8236 specification; Quin, Rafferty &amp; Tomlinson, <em>Safe Dance Practice</em>; the AQA dance anthology set works.</p>
</TopicPage>
